.TH TERMCAP 3
.SH NAME
tgetent, tgetnum, tgetflag, tgetstr, tgoto, tputs \- terminal independent operation routines
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
char PC;
char *BC;
char *UP;
short ospeed;

int
tgetent(bp, name)
char *bp, *name;

int
tgetnum(id)
char *id;

int
tgetflag(id)
char *id;

char *
tgetstr(id, area)
char *id, **area;

char *
tgoto(cm, destcol, destline)
char *cm;
int destcol, destline;

tputs(cp, affcnt, outc)
char *cp;
int affcnt;
int (*outc)();
.fi
.PP
cc ... \-ltermcap
.SH DESCRIPTION
These read what a terminal can do, and the codes that make it do it,
from the terminal capability data base, /etc/termcap, so that one
program works on every terminal described there.
.PP
.B tgetent
finds the entry for the terminal
.I name
(usually from getenv("TERM")) and keeps it in the buffer
.IR bp ,
a char array of 1024 bytes that must stay there while the other
functions are used. It returns 1 if it found the entry, 0 if there is
none, and \-1 if it could not open the termcap file. A tc= capability
brings in the entry it names, as many as 16 deep.
.PP
If the environment has TERMCAP, and it begins with a /, it is the path
of the file to use instead of /etc/termcap. If it does not begin with a
/ and it is an entry with
.I name
among its names, that entry is used, and no file is read.
.PP
Each capability has a name of two characters,
.IR id .
.B tgetnum
returns the value of a number, as tgetnum("co") for the columns, or
\-1 if the terminal does not have it.
.B tgetflag
returns 1 if the terminal has a flag, such as "am", and 0 if not.
.B tgetstr
returns a string, as tgetstr("cl", &p) for the code that clears the
screen, or NULL if there is none. The escapes in it (\eE, ^X, \en,
\e072 and the rest) are turned into the characters they stand for,
which are put at *\fIarea\fR, and *\fIarea\fR is moved past them,
ready for the next.
.PP
.B tgoto
fills in the cursor motion string
.IR cm ,
the "cm" capability, to go to column
.I destcol
and line
.I destline
(counting from 0), and returns the result, in a static area. Where
.I cm
sends a position as a single character (%. or %+), tgoto avoids sending
a NUL, ^D or newline, which could be lost on the way to the terminal: it
goes one further and then comes back, with UP or BC (a backspace if BC
is not set). It returns "OOPS" if
.I cm
is NULL or uses a % it does not know.
.PP
.B tputs
puts out the string
.IR cp ,
one character at a time through the function
.IR outc ,
which takes the character as its argument, as putchar does. A
number at the start of the string, as in "50\e33[H" or "3.5*", is a delay
in milliseconds that the terminal needs after it: tputs makes the delay
by sending pad characters (PC), as many as take that long at the
output speed
.IR ospeed .
With a *, the delay is for each line affected, and
.I affcnt
is the number of lines. Set
.I ospeed
from the terminal's speed (the CBAUD bits of termio's c_cflag), or
leave it 0 for no padding.
.PP
The program sets the variables from the terminal's entry: UP from the
"up" capability, BC from "bc", and PC from the first character of
"pc" (it is NUL until then).
.SH EXAMPLES
To clear the screen:
.PP
.nf
	char bp[1024], area[100], *p = area, *cl;
	int putch();

	if (tgetent(bp, getenv("TERM")) == 1 && (cl = tgetstr("cl", &p)))
		tputs(cl, tgetnum("li"), putch);

	putch(c) { putchar(c); }
.fi
.SH "RETURN VALUES"
tgetent returns 1, 0 or \-1, tgetnum the number or \-1, tgetflag 1 or 0,
tgetstr the string or NULL, and tgoto the string or "OOPS", as described
above.
.SH FILES
.TP 20
/etc/termcap
the data base of terminals
.TP
/usr/lib/cc/libtermcap.a
the library
.SH SEE ALSO
clear(1), tset(1), termcap(5)
.SH HISTORY
The termcap functions first appeared in 4.0BSD. The terminal capability
data base was first written by Bill Joy at Berkeley, for the screen
editor vi.
.SH BUGS
The entry of a terminal, with the entries tc= brings in, must fit in
1024 bytes.
.PP
tgoto's static result is overwritten by the next call.
